window.OVERVIEW_QUESTION_GUIDE = {"cycles": {"c1": "<div class=\"ov-map\"><table class=\"ov-question-map\"><caption class=\"sr-only\">Cycle 1 parts and questions by step</caption><thead><tr><th id=\"ov-c1-step1-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 1</span>Investigate</th><th id=\"ov-c1-step2-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 2</span>Plan</th><th id=\"ov-c1-step3-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 3</span>Act</th><th id=\"ov-c1-step4-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 4</span>Reflect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td headers=\"ov-c1-step1-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 1: Investigate</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c1-A\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-A\">Part A</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Data and equity gap</p><h5>I. Initial data</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>Table task</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-A-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-A-item-1\">Initial quantitative data</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-A-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-A-item-2\">Select one student group</a></p><h5>II. Extended data</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>Table task</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-A-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-A-item-3\">Qualitative evidence</a></p><h5>III. Vision, mission, and goals</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-A-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-A-item-4\">Investigate school commitments</a></p><h5>IV. Equity gap analysis</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-A-item-5\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-A-item-5\">Select the gap from the data</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-A-item-6\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-A-item-6\">Explain qualitative sources</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-A-item-7\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-A-item-7\">Connect findings across sources</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-A-item-8\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-A-item-8\">Define the gap using research</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>5.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-A-item-9\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-A-item-9\">Connect the gap to school commitments</a></p></section></td><td headers=\"ov-c1-step2-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 2: Plan</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c1-B\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-B\">Part B</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Factors and problem statement</p><h5>I. Institutional/structural factors</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-B-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-B-item-1\">Identify contributing factors</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-B-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-B-item-2\">Explain their impact</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-B-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-B-item-3\">Use research to examine factors</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-B-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-B-item-4\">Identify the educational need</a></p><h5>II. Problem statement</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>5.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-B-item-5\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-B-item-5\">Develop a feasible problem statement</a></p></section></td><td headers=\"ov-c1-step3-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 3: Act</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c1-C\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-C\">Part C</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Improvement plan</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-C-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-C-item-1\">Propose strategies</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-C-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-C-item-2\">Explain how they address the problem</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-C-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-C-item-3\">Connect strategies, factors, and goals</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-C-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-C-item-4\">Apply targeted feedback</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>5.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-C-item-5\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-C-item-5\">Build participation; anticipate implications</a></p></section></td><td headers=\"ov-c1-step4-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 4: Reflect</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c1-D\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-D\">Part D</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Reflection</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-D-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-D-item-1\">Explain feedback choices and influence</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-D-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-D-item-2\">Analyze learning, strengths, and growth</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c1-D-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c1-D-item-3\">Plan professional learning</a></p></section></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note\">Plain-language summaries with current template numbering. Roman numerals identify sections; letters identify subparts. Unnumbered evidence tasks remain unnumbered. The official template and handbook govern.</p><section class=\"ov-templates\" id=\"ov-c1-templates\"><h3>Current blank templates</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original pages from the supplied CTC Version 09 templates, in part and page order.</p><div class=\"ov-template-gallery\"><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"A\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 1, Part A: Data and equity gap, page 1 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 1, Part A: Data and equity gap, page 1 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-A-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 1, Part A: Data and equity gap, page 1 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part A: Data and equity gap</strong> <span>Page 1 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S1_A_DataCollectionEquityGapAnalysis.docx\" download>Part A Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"A\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 1, Part A: Data and equity gap, page 2 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 1, Part A: Data and equity gap, page 2 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-A-2.png\" alt=\"Cycle 1, Part A: Data and equity gap, page 2 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part A: Data and equity gap</strong> <span>Page 2 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S1_A_DataCollectionEquityGapAnalysis.docx\" download>Part A Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"B\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 1, Part B: Factors and problem statement, page 1 of 1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 1, Part B: Factors and problem statement, page 1 of 1\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-B-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 1, Part B: Factors and problem statement, page 1 of 1. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part B: Factors and problem statement</strong> <span>Page 1 of 1</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S2_B_ContribFactorsAndProbStatement.docx\" download>Part B Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"C\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 1, Part C: Improvement plan, page 1 of 1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 1, Part C: Improvement plan, page 1 of 1\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-C-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 1, Part C: Improvement plan, page 1 of 1. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part C: Improvement plan</strong> <span>Page 1 of 1</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S3_C_PlanSchoolImprovementPromotEquity.docx\" download>Part C Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"D\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 1, Part D: Reflection, page 1 of 1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 1, Part D: Reflection, page 1 of 1\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-D-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 1, Part D: Reflection, page 1 of 1. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part D: Reflection</strong> <span>Page 1 of 1</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S4_D_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Part D Word template</a></figure></div></section><section class=\"ov-preparation\"><h3>Before you begin</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Preparation guidance for the required work, not additional submission items. Possible people and records are suggestions; select those relevant to your setting.</p><dl><div><dt>Materials and access</dt><dd>Read Parts A-D together with the handbook before collecting evidence. Locate the three most recent years for an eligible state indicator, with group definitions, measures, denominators, and source dates. Gather the adopted mission, goals, and relevant improvement documents.</dd></div><div><dt>People to involve</dt><dd>Identify someone who can explain the data; students, families, educators, or support staff whose perspectives can illuminate the concern; and an administrator or educational leader who can give informed feedback on the proposal. These are possible perspectives, not a required interview roster.</dd></div><div><dt>Conversations to have</dt><dd>Clarify what the numerical measure includes. Ask about the selected group&#x27;s experiences, access to support, and school processes. Examine where accounts agree or differ. Later, ask leaders to challenge the proposed strategies&#x27; fit, resources, and feasibility.</dd></div><div><dt>Sequence and records</dt><dd>Arrange access and permissions early. Keep sources, dates, populations, methods, findings, and limitations together. Develop the problem from the findings before choosing strategies; preserve the feedback and resulting revisions for Parts C and D.</dd></div><div><dt>What to anticipate</dt><dd>You need at least three different qualitative sources, not simply three labels. A proposal is sufficient for this cycle; do not invent implementation results. The school&#x27;s stated commitment is not proof that the intended students received a service.</dd></div></dl></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c1-step1\"><h3>Step 1: Investigate</h3><p>Move from a numerical difference to an evidence-supported understanding of an equity concern. Quantitative data identify patterns; qualitative inquiry investigates experiences and conditions. Bring them together with school commitments without treating a disparity as proof of its cause.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubrics: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-1-1\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-1-1\">1.1</a>, <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-1-2\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-1-2\">1.2</a>, <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-1-3\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-1-3\">1.3</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c1-A\" data-part=\"A\"><h4>Part A: Data and equity gap</h4><p>Present the data and school commitments, then integrate the findings into an equity-gap analysis. The four sections have different jobs: collect, document, compare, and explain.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S1_A_DataCollectionEquityGapAnalysis.docx\" download>Current Part A template</a></p><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">I. Initial data</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-A-item-1\" data-question=\"Table task\"><h6>Table task What do three years of quantitative data show?</h6><p>Choose one eligible state indicator and present the three most recent years for the school and its student groups. Identify the measure, years, populations, and sources so the comparisons can be interpreted. Part A IV.1 explains the pattern and rationale.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-A-item-2\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. Which specific student group will you investigate?</h6><p>Name one group clearly and consistently. This short response identifies the group; Part A IV.1 develops the numerical analysis and the reason for selecting the gap. Use a group that is identifiable in the data and appropriate for the chosen indicator.</p></section><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">II. Extended data</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-A-item-3\" data-question=\"Table task\"><h6>Table task What do the qualitative sources reveal?</h6><p>Record what each qualitative source revealed and explain what it contributes to understanding the selected group and indicator. A source is the person or material; an interview is a method; a finding is what was learned; analysis explains its significance and limits. Listing interview participants completes only the first part of this task.</p></section><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">III. Vision, mission, and goals</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-A-item-4\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. What are the school&#x27;s adopted vision, mission, and/or goals?</h6><p>Investigate the school&#x27;s adopted vision, mission, or goals and document the commitments relevant to your selected population. Explain why those commitments matter for that group. This question establishes the school&#x27;s intentions; Part A IV.5 develops the comparison between those intentions and the equity-gap evidence.</p></section><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">IV. Equity gap analysis</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-A-item-5\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. Why have you selected this equity gap for further investigation?</h6><p>Explain why this student group and equity gap deserve investigation. Interpret the three-year quantitative pattern, explain why you collected the additional numerical data, and show what those data contribute to understanding equity at the school. A list of percentages is incomplete without the reasoning that connects them to your choice.</p></section><p class=\"source-note\">Template/handbook reading note: IV.1 refers to quantitative data in \"Section II,\" but both documents place Initial Data Collection in Section I. Address the quantitative task using the actual initial-data section; preserve the official prompt in your submission.</p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-A-item-6\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. Why did you select these qualitative sources, and what did each contribute?</h6><p>For each qualitative source, explain the perspective it provides, what you learned, and how that finding adds to your understanding of the selected group and indicator. Explain why the source was useful. A biography of each participant or a list of interviews does not show the contribution of the evidence.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-A-item-7\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How do the qualitative findings relate to the quantitative findings?</h6><p>Move from individual accounts to patterns across the qualitative evidence. Identify which sources support each pattern, then explain how it relates to the numerical trend. Include evidence that complicates your explanation. The task is to connect different kinds of evidence, not repeat every interview or assume that all sources confirm the same story.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-A-item-8\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. What is the equity gap, and how do evidence and research help explain it?</h6><p>State precisely what the equity gap is for the selected group and indicator. Bring together the numerical disparity and the qualitative findings that help interpret it. Then explain how relevant research informs that interpretation. Research should contribute an idea or finding you can apply and examine, not serve as a quotation added after the conclusion.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-A-item-9\" data-question=\"5.\"><h6>5. How does the equity gap relate to the school&#x27;s commitments?</h6><p>Compare the equity-gap findings with specific school commitments documented in Part A III. Explain what the school promises for this population, what the evidence suggests about students&#x27; experiences, and where the two align or appear to differ. Repeating the mission or stating that the gap conflicts with it leaves the comparison unfinished.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-1-1\"><h5>Rubric 1.1: Quantitative inquiry</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.1 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Missing three-year data, relevant patterns, or an appropriate group.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Minimal analysis; group choice or rationale unclear.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Analyze three years for one state indicator, explain equity patterns, and justify the selected student group.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of 3, plus additional indicator-linked data that clarify group differences.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of 3 and 4, plus relevant research informing the equity interpretation.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 1.1</summary><p>Essential Question: Based on the chosen California state indicator, how well does the candidate select and analyze quantitative data sources across the three most recent years, identify patterns and/or trends related to equity, and choose an appropriate student group?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.1 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not include quantitative data across the three most recent years. OR Patterns and trends are not identified or are irrelevant. OR Candidate does not select a student group to investigate OR selects a student group not appropriate for the identified indicator.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate selects a California state indicator* and minimally analyzes quantitative data, vaguely identifying patterns and/or trends related to school equity. Candidate is not clear about which student group they will investigate or why the student group was selected.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate selects a California state indicator* and analyzes quantitative data across the three most recent years, clearly identifying general patterns and/or trends related to school equity. Candidate clearly identifies a student group to investigate further and provides a rationale for why this student group was selected.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explores additional data linked to the indicator that clearly informs their understanding of patterns and/or trends to further clarify group differences within the state indicator selected (e.g., demographic data, ethnicity, gender, language).</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites relevant research that informs their understanding (patterns and/or trends related to equity) of the determined equity issue for the chosen student group.</p></section></div><p>Note: *California state indicator: Chronic Absenteeism, Suspension Rate, English Learner Progress, Graduation Rate, Academic Performance, College/Career Readiness Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Data Tables and Written Narrative: Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis (up to 5 pages of responses to prompts, exclusive of quantitative and qualitative data tables) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 10</a>.</p></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-1-2\"><h5>Rubric 1.2: Qualitative inquiry</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.2 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Fewer than three analyzed qualitative sources, or no relevant connection to numerical findings.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>At least three sources; analysis and connections are minimal.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Three relevant sources analyzed; relationships to numerical findings and the equity issue explained.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of 3, plus appropriate additional qualitative data and a complete connected analysis.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of 3 and 4, plus collection responsive to context, cultural sensitivity, and diverse views.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 1.2</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate collect and analyze relevant qualitative data from a range of at least three different qualitative* data sources and explain their relationship of the findings from the analysis to the quantitative** data findings and the identified student group equity issue?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.2 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not provide or analyze three qualitative* data sources. OR Candidate provides no or irrelevant information about the connection among qualitative and quantitative data** findings and the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate collects and minimally analyzes a range of qualitative* data from at least three sources. Candidate minimally connects the qualitative data and findings to the quantitative data** findings for the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate collects and analyzes a range of relevant qualitative* data from three different sources. Candidate clearly explains the relationship among the findings from the analyses of the qualitative data, the quantitative data,** and the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate collects additional qualitative data as appropriate to deepen their understanding of the California state indicator chosen and the student group equity issue, and provides a complete analysis of the relationship between their qualitative and quantitative data findings and the identified student group equity issue.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains how their qualitative data collection strategy is responsive to the complex context in which they are working and demonstrates cultural sensitivity and an appreciation for diverse viewpoints.</p></section></div><p>Notes: *Qualitative: Collected through methods like interviews, focus groups, and observations, this data is subjective, rich in context, and commonly analyzed by identifying themes, patterns, or through coding (e.g., responses from interviews with students, faculty, or families/guardians; notes from observations; document analysis) **Quantitative: Numerical data expressing a certain quantity, amount, or range. Usually, there are measurement units associated with the data (e.g., meters, degrees, score points). Arithmetic and statistical operations may be applied to quantitative data. Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Data Tables and Written Narrative: Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis (up to 5 pages of responses to prompts, exclusive of quantitative and qualitative data tables) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3B</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 11</a>.</p></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-1-3\"><h5>Rubric 1.3: Equity gap analysis</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.3 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Missing analysis, patterns, equity-gap description, or school-goal alignment.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Minimal or unclear analysis, gap description, or alignment.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Clear integrated analysis, patterns, equity issue, and school-goal alignment.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of 3, plus thorough synthesis and a sophisticated understanding of the disparity.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of 3 and 4, plus research explained for this group&#x27;s equity gap.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 1.3</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate conduct an equity gap analysis based on the chosen California state indicator to inform their understanding of the equity issues for the identified student group? How does this equity gap align with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.3 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate identifies an equity issue with no evidence of quantitative or qualitative data analysis. OR Candidate does not identify any patterns and/or trends in the equity gap analysis provided. OR Candidate does not provide a description of an equity gap for the student group. OR Candidate does not identify the alignment between the equity gap for a student group at the school and the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate identifies an equity issue for an identified student group based on minimal quantitative or qualitative data analysis. Candidate identifies patterns and/or trends that are not clear in the equity gap analysis provided or does not clearly describe the identified patterns and/or trends. Candidate provides a minimal description of an equity gap for the student group. Candidate minimally describes the alignment between the equity gap for a student group at the school and the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate identifies an equity issue for an identified student group clearly based on their quantitative and qualitative data analysis for the chosen state indicator. Candidate clearly describes patterns and/or trends across the quantitative and qualitative data analysis in the equity gap analysis provided. Candidate clearly identifies the alignment between the equity gap for the identified student group and the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate conducts a thorough equity gap analysis, describing a clear connection from quantitative data findings to supportive qualitative data findings, and provides a sophisticated understanding of the equity disparity identified for the student group.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites and explains how research informs their understanding of the equity gap for the specific student group.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Data Tables and Written Narrative: Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis (up to 5 pages of responses to prompts, exclusive of quantitative and qualitative data tables) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C CAPE Standard 3; Elements 3B, 3C</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 12</a>.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c1-step2\"><h3>Step 2: Plan</h3><p>Use the investigation to identify potential contributing factors and a focused problem the school can feasibly address. Institutional and structural factors are practices, policies, arrangements, or conditions, not labels for what is supposedly wrong with students or families.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubrics: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-1-4\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-1-4\">1.4</a>, <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-1-5\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-1-5\">1.5</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c1-B\" data-part=\"B\"><h4>Part B: Factors and problem statement</h4><p>Distinguish the possible contributing factors, the educational need, and the problem statement. The problem should follow from the inquiry, not be a disguised list of preferred programs.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S2_B_ContribFactorsAndProbStatement.docx\" download>Current Part B template</a></p><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">I. Institutional/structural factors</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-B-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. What factors may have created or contributed to this gap?</h6><p>Identify plausible conditions that may have created or contributed to the single equity gap. Draw each factor from the Part A findings and explain the school practice, arrangement, or process involved. &#x27;Potential&#x27; matters: the evidence should justify investigating the factor without pretending that the inquiry has proved its causal effect.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-B-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. How might those factors affect this group&#x27;s learning or well-being?</h6><p>Explain how each identified factor could affect this group&#x27;s learning or well-being. Describe the pathway from the condition to a student experience and its possible consequence. Keep the explanation tied to Part A evidence; naming a factor again or saying it causes the gap does not explain its impact.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-B-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How does research inform your understanding of these factors?</h6><p>Use research to examine the contributing factors identified from your findings. For each relevant study, explain what it found, how that finding relates to your factor, and why the connection is appropriate for this group and setting. Embed citations in the response as directed by the template and handbook.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-B-item-4\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. What educational need emerges from the investigation?</h6><p>Identify what the selected group needs in order to experience more equitable learning or well-being. Describe the unmet condition indicated by the inquiry before naming a program. Supports, staffing, materials, or opportunities may be relevant, but explain the need they would address and its relationship to the single equity gap.</p></section><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">II. Problem statement</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-B-item-5\" data-question=\"5.\"><h6>5. What focused, feasible problem should the school address?</h6><p>Describe the single equity gap the school needs to address, keeping the indicator, student group, and learning or well-being need explicit. Draw the statement from the inquiry and contributing factors. Explain why the problem is responsive to the group and feasible to address within the school&#x27;s actual context.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-1-4\"><h5>Rubric 1.4: Contributing factors</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.4 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Biased, superficial, or irrelevant factors; or missing research/citation connections.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Factors minimally connected; research citations unrelated to the gap.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Factors grounded in the inquiry, including institutional/structural conditions, with relevant research.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of 3, plus detailed explanations of several factors supported by Step 1.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of 3 and 4, plus sophisticated research-based understanding of systemic causes.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 1.4</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate determine contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, that created or added to the identified equity gap affecting a student group and cite the research supporting their determination?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.4 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate identifies contributing factors that are biased, superficial, or irrelevant to the equity gap analysis. OR Candidate does not cite research and/or neglects to draw connections between research and contributing factors.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate identifies potential contributing factors and minimally describes how they relate to the equity gap analysis. Candidate attempts to draw connections between research and contributing factors, but citations are not related to the equity gap.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate clearly uses the equity gap analysis and findings from the quantitative and qualitative data analyses to determine contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, that created or added to an equity gap affecting a student group. Candidate cites relevant research to support potential contributing factors that create or add to the identified equity gap affecting a student group.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains in detail (with supporting evidence from Step 1) how several contributing factors can create or add to equity differences or disparities for a student group.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate demonstrates a sophisticated, research-based understanding of the systemic, institutional, or structural causes of the identified single equity gap for a group of students at the school.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Written Narrative: Contributing Factors and Problem Statement (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1A CAPE Standard 2; Element 2A CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 15</a>.</p></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-1-5\"><h5>Rubric 1.5: Problem statement</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.5 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Problem not drawn from the inquiry/factors, or unresponsive to the group.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Connection to achievement/well-being unclear; only partially responsive.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Feasible, responsive problem clearly drawn from the inquiry and factors.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of 3, plus why the problem is responsive and feasible in this school&#x27;s context.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of 3 and 4, plus relevant practices or research from similar settings/groups.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 1.5</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate use the equity gap analysis and identification of potential contributing factors to develop a feasible problem statement related to student achievement and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.5 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not use the equity gap analysis or potential contributing factors to develop a problem statement. OR Candidate\u2019s problem statement is not responsive to the needs of the student group.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate attempts to use the equity gap analysis and potential contributing factors to develop a problem statement, but it is not clear how the problem statement supports student achievement and/or well-being of the student group. Candidate\u2019s problem statement is only partially responsive to the needs of the student group.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate develops a feasible problem statement related to student achievement and/or well-being of the student group that clearly draws from the equity gap analysis and potential contributing factors identified. Candidate\u2019s problem statement is clearly responsive to the needs of the student group.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains why their problem statement is responsive and feasible for the school\u2019s culture and context.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites relevant evidence-based practices or research on how the area of educational need has been addressed in other school settings to improve achievement and/or well-being for similar student groups.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Written Narrative: Contributing Factors and Problem Statement (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 16</a>.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c1-step3\"><h3>Step 3: Act</h3><p>Develop strategies that respond to the established problem, seek informed leadership feedback, and use it to refine the proposal. Explain participation and implementation implications. This cycle requires a justified proposal, not evidence that you implemented it or achieved results.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubrics: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-1-6\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-1-6\">1.6</a>, <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-1-7\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-1-7\">1.7</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c1-C\" data-part=\"C\"><h4>Part C: Improvement plan</h4><p>Explain the proposal and how leadership feedback improved it. Keep planned actions separate from actions already completed.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S3_C_PlanSchoolImprovementPromotEquity.docx\" download>Current Part C template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-C-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. What improvement strategies do you propose?</h6><p>Describe the strategies you propose and how each would operate at the school. Connect them to the equity gap and explain the activities, responsibilities, and application well enough for the reader to understand the proposal. This is a plan for improvement; distinguish proposed actions from actions already completed.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-C-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. How would the strategies address the problem?</h6><p>Explain why each proposed strategy is expected to address the problem statement. Describe the mechanism: what would change in students&#x27; access or experience, and how that change relates to the identified need. Use relevant evidence to justify the expectation without presenting a hoped-for outcome as an established result.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-C-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How do the strategies connect to contributing factors and school goals?</h6><p>Make two connections for each strategy: explain which contributing factor it addresses and how it aligns with a specific school commitment. Show the chain from finding to factor to action to school goal. Explain any important factor the chosen strategies do not address.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-C-item-4\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. How did you use targeted feedback to improve the proposal?</h6><p>Show what feedback you received on each proposed strategy and what you changed, or would change, because of it. Explain the reason for each decision, including a recommendation you did not adopt. Keep the sequence visible so the reader can follow the proposal from feedback through revision.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-C-item-5\" data-question=\"5.\"><h6>5. How will you build participation and anticipate implementation implications?</h6><p>Explain how different educational partners would become involved in the adjusted proposal and what practical consequences or constraints you anticipate. Specify opportunities for people to influence the plan, not only receive an announcement. Connect likely implementation difficulties to the steps you would take to address them.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-1-6\"><h5>Rubric 1.6: Strategies and alignment</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.6 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>No informed improvement strategy, or no school-goal alignment.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Minimal connection to findings/problem; partial goal alignment.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Informed strategies respond to findings, factors, and problem, with clear goal alignment.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of 3, plus strategic equity focus and a contextually responsive approach.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of 3 and 4, plus relevant research-based implementation evidence for this group/school.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 1.6</summary><p>Essential Question: How are the strategy(ies) proposed for equitable school improvement for the student group well informed by the findings of the equity gap analysis, including contributing factors, and responsive to the problem statement? How is the proposed strategy(ies) aligned to the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.6 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not propose strategy(ies) for equitable school improvement informed by the findings. OR Proposed strategy(ies) are not aligned with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate\u2019s proposed strategy(ies) are minimally informed by the findings and provide only general reference to the equity gap analysis, contributing factors, and/or problem statement. Proposed strategy(ies) are partially aligned with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate\u2019s proposed strategy(ies) for equitable school improvement for the student group are well informed by the findings of the equity gap analysis and the contributing factors are responsive to the problem statement. Proposed strategy(ies) are clearly aligned with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides relevant strategy(ies) that strategically focus on equitable student and school improvement and represent a contextually responsive approach to addressing the equity issue or educational need.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides research- based evidence of the relevance of the proposed strategy(ies) and their implementation for improving student achievement and/or well-being for the specific student group and school.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part C: Written Narrative: Planning for School Improvement and Promoting Equity (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1A CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 18</a>.</p></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-1-7\"><h5>Rubric 1.7: Feedback and feasibility</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.7 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>No applied feedback, meaningful buy-in explanation, or anticipated implications.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Feedback, adjustments, participation, or implications are vague or inappropriate.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Clear applied feedback, appropriate buy-in steps, and realistic implications.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of 3, plus additional feedback on revisions and a detailed, diverse-partner communication plan.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of 3 and 4, plus planned coaching to examine bias and underlying disadvantage.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 1.7</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate apply the feedback received from administrators or educational leaders familiar with the school culture and context and describe next steps for creating educational partner buy-in and potential implications for the adjusted set of strategy(ies)?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.7 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not apply feedback from an administrator/supervisor/ educational leader to adjust or strengthen their proposed strategy(ies). OR Candidate states their plan(s) to communicate the proposed strategy(ies) with little or no explanation of steps for buy-in from educational partner(s). OR Candidate does not identify anticipated implications for proposed strategy(ies).</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate vaguely describes feedback received from an administrator/supervisor/ educational leader and makes irrelevant and/or inappropriate adjustments to proposed strategy(ies). Candidate vaguely describes their plan(s) to communicate the proposed strategy(ies) with educational partner(s); it is not clear that educational partner(s) will have the opportunity to develop buy-in. Candidate does not clearly describe anticipated, realistic implications that may be encountered at the school related to implementation of proposed strategy(ies).</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate clearly applies feedback received from an administrator/supervisor/ educational leader to adjust or strengthen proposed strategy(ies). Candidate clearly provides relevant and appropriate next steps for creating buy-in and communicating with educational partner(s) to address the equity gap and learning need(s). Candidate clearly describes anticipated, realistic implications that may be encountered at the school related to the implementation of proposed strategy(ies).</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate seeks additional rounds of feedback from other administrators or educational partners on the revised strategy(ies) and develops a detailed strategic plan to communicate and share the plan with a diverse range of educational partners to ensure they are proposing a workable/feasible approach to addressing the equity gap and learning need.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate plans to coach educational partners to examine and address potential biases that could impact student learning and/or well- being due to identified equity gaps, including those specifically related to sources of education disadvantage or discrimination, and is transparent about the potential underlying contributing factors.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part C: Written Narrative: Planning for School Improvement and Promoting Equity (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1B, 1C CAPE Standard 2; Element 2A CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5B, 5C CAPE Standard 6; Elements 6A, 6B</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=23\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 19</a>.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c1-step4\"><h3>Step 4: Reflect</h3><p>Examine your leadership using decisions, feedback, and evidence from the cycle. Explain what you learned about equitable leadership, where your practice needs development, and how you will continue learning.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubric: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-1-8\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-1-8\">1.8</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c1-D\" data-part=\"D\"><h4>Part D: Reflection</h4><p>Use specific cycle evidence to examine your own leadership. The current template has three questions.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S4_D_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Current Part D template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-D-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. Why did you choose these feedback partners, and how did their input influence you?</h6><p>Explain why you chose these feedback partners and how their feedback changed your approach to gaining participation. Connect the partner&#x27;s relevant expertise to a specific recommendation, decision, and lesson for leadership. Praise for a leader&#x27;s qualities does not by itself explain the effect of the feedback.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-D-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. What did you learn about equitable leadership, your strengths, and your growth needs?</h6><p>Explain what you learned about equity leadership, identify strengths and areas for growth, and show how your work addressed this group&#x27;s equity needs. Support the reflection with specific evidence from Steps 1, 2, or 3. Describe changes in your understanding and decisions, not only the activities you completed or traits you believe you possess.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c1-D-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. What professional learning will you pursue because of this experience?</h6><p>Turn the learning from this cycle into specific professional goals and future steps. Identify the skill you need to develop, what you will do to develop it, and how you will judge your progress. A new committee role or another data project is a learning opportunity; explain the leadership growth it is intended to produce.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-1-8\"><h5>Rubric 1.8: Leadership learning</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.8 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Missing feedback rationale, equity reflection, specific evidence, or strengths/growth.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Limited rationale and vague reflection, evidence, or growth discussion.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Clear feedback rationale, evidence-supported equity reflection, strengths, and future growth.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of 3, plus how social, economic, or cultural context shapes equity leadership.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of 3 and 4, plus skills learned across the cycle and their future use.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 1.8</summary><p>Essential Question: How did the leadership feedback shape the candidate\u2019s equity-focused strategy, build educational partner buy- in, and support their growth in addressing equity needs and gaps for the identified student group? How does the candidate reflect on their current strengths and identify future areas for growth in equity leadership?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"1.8 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not discuss or only mentions their selected administrator/supervisor/ educational leader and/or the importance of educational partner buy-in is not discussed in the reflection. OR Candidate does not reflect on their growth as an equity- driven leader and/or does not address the equity needs or equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site. OR Candidate does not refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. OR Candidate does not discuss strengths or areas for future professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate provides limited insight into why they selected the administrator/supervisor/ educational leader for feedback and how that feedback impacted their approach to build buy-in. Candidate vaguely reflects on their growth as an equity- driven leader and minimally discusses their ability to address the equity needs and/or equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site, referring to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Candidate does not clearly explain their current strengths and/or vaguely mentions future areas for growth in equity leadership.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate clearly explains their rationale for selecting the administrator/supervisor/ educational leader to provide feedback on the selected strategy(ies) and how that feedback impacted their approach to build buy-in from school personnel. Candidate clearly reflects on their growth as an equity- driven leader and discusses their ability to address the equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site, referring to specific, relevant evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Candidate clearly explains their current strengths and identifies future areas for growth in equity leadership.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate\u2019s reflection demonstrates how the school context\u2014 including social, economic, or cultural contexts\u2014 impacts their approach to providing equity- driven leadership.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate utilizes experiences from Steps 1, 2, and 3 in confronting equity issues and gaps to describe what leadership skills have been learned and how those skills may be used in the future.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part D: Reflective Narrative (up to 5 pages of written or up to 5 minutes of video explanation) CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5B CAPE Standard 6; Element 6A</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 21</a>.</p></section></section><p><a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Continue to the detailed Cycle 1 guide and examples</a></p>", "c2": "<div class=\"ov-map\"><table class=\"ov-question-map\"><caption class=\"sr-only\">Cycle 2 parts and questions by step</caption><thead><tr><th id=\"ov-c2-step1-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 1</span>Investigate</th><th id=\"ov-c2-step2-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 2</span>Plan</th><th id=\"ov-c2-step3-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 3</span>Act</th><th id=\"ov-c2-step4-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 4</span>Reflect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td headers=\"ov-c2-step1-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 1: Investigate</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c2-A\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-A\">Part A</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Context, focus, and community</p><h5>I. Professional learning</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-A-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-A-item-1\">Analyze collaborative professional learning</a></p><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> Formal training in the past three years</li><li><strong>b.</strong> Current professional learning and impact</li><li><strong>c.</strong> Communities of practice and impact</li><li><strong>d.</strong> Adult-learning processes and research</li></ul><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-A-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-A-item-2\">Identify implications for facilitation</a></p><h5>II. Educational focus and membership</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-A-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-A-item-3\">Select a data-supported educational focus</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-A-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-A-item-4\">Explain membership and inclusion</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-A-item-5\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-A-item-5\">Compare member and school demographics</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-A-item-6\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-A-item-6\">Explain your relationship to each member</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>5.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-A-item-7\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-A-item-7\">Explain likely benefits for students</a></p></section></td><td headers=\"ov-c2-step2-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 2: Plan</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c2-B\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-B\">Part B</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Planning agenda</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-B-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-B-item-1\">School commitments</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-B-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-B-item-2\">Data review</a></p><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> Data sources</li><li><strong>b.</strong> Discussion of data</li></ul><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-B-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-B-item-3\">Problem of practice</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-B-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-B-item-4\">Strategy to address the problem</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>5.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-B-item-5\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-B-item-5\">Implementation plan</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>6.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-B-item-6\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-B-item-6\">Next steps and action items</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c2-C\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-C\">Part C</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Planning minutes</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-C-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-C-item-1\">School commitments</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-C-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-C-item-2\">Data analysis</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-C-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-C-item-3\">Problem of practice</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-C-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-C-item-4\">Selected strategy</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>5.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-C-item-5\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-C-item-5\">Implementation agreements</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>6.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-C-item-6\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-C-item-6\">Next steps and action items</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c2-D\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-D\">Part D</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Problem and strategy</p><h5>II. Problem of practice</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-D-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-D-item-1\">Analyze data and select the problem jointly</a></p><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> How did you work together to analyze the student data?</li><li><strong>b.</strong> How did the group select a problem within the educational focus?</li><li><strong>c.</strong> How could changing current practice improve student learning or well-being?</li></ul><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-D-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-D-item-2\">Examine differences among student groups</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-D-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-D-item-3\">Identify changes in current practice</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-D-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-D-item-4\">Connect goals, standards, and group differences</a></p><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> How does the problem connect to school vision, mission, or goals?</li><li><strong>b.</strong> How does it relate to relevant standards or curriculum frameworks?</li><li><strong>c.</strong> How does it address differences in student-group performance or well-being?</li></ul><h5>III. Strategy</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-D-item-5\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-D-item-5\">Select one evidence-based strategy jointly</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-D-item-6\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-D-item-6\">Explain rationale, learning, and challenges</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-D-item-7\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-D-item-7\">Explain expected student benefits</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-D-item-8\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-D-item-8\">Plan monitoring and analysis</a></p></section></td><td headers=\"ov-c2-step3-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 3: Act</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c2-E\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-E\">Part E</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Implementation agendas</p><p class=\"ov-map-note\">Questions 1-3 repeat for each of the three meetings.</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-E-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-E-item-1\">Early returns and new data</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-E-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-E-item-2\">Meeting summary</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-E-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-E-item-3\">Next steps</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c2-F\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-F\">Part F</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Implementation minutes</p><p class=\"ov-map-note\">Record these fields for each of the three meetings.</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>a.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-F-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-F-item-1\">What is going well?</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>b.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-F-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-F-item-2\">What needs adjustment?</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>c.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-F-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-F-item-3\">Participant reflections</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong></strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-F-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-F-item-4\">Meeting summary and next steps</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c2-G\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-G\">Part G</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Collaborative work products</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong></strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-G-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-G-item-1\">Submit the actual products used by the group</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c2-H\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-H\">Part H</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Implementation video clips</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>Clip 1</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-H-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-H-item-1\">Purpose and data review</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>Clip 2</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-H-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-H-item-2\">Professional collaboration</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>Clip 3</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-H-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-H-item-3\">Results and next steps</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c2-I\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-I\">Part I</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Video commentary</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-I-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-I-item-1\">Set the meeting purpose</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-I-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-I-item-2\">Evaluate implementation data</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-I-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-I-item-3\">Facilitate agreements and shared understanding</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-I-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-I-item-4\">Support viewpoints, feedback, focus, and progress</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>5.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-I-item-5\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-I-item-5\">Determine next steps together</a></p></section></td><td headers=\"ov-c2-step4-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 4: Reflect</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c2-J\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-J\">Part J</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Reflection</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-J-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-J-item-1\">Connect results, feedback, leadership, and growth</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-J-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-J-item-2\">Analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-J-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-J-item-3\">Explain school context and your response</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c2-J-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c2-J-item-4\">Set professional learning goals</a></p></section></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note\">Plain-language summaries with current template numbering. Roman numerals identify sections; letters identify subparts. Unnumbered evidence tasks remain unnumbered. The official template and handbook govern.</p><section class=\"ov-templates\" id=\"ov-c2-templates\"><h3>Current blank templates</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original pages from the supplied CTC Version 09 templates, in part and page order.</p><div class=\"ov-template-gallery\"><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"A\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part A: Context, focus, and community, page 1 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part A: Context, focus, and community, page 1 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-A-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part A: Context, focus, and community, page 1 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part A: Context, focus, and community</strong> <span>Page 1 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S1_A_ContextEdFocusCommunityPrac.docx\" download>Part A Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"A\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part A: Context, focus, and community, page 2 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part A: Context, focus, and community, page 2 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-A-2.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part A: Context, focus, and community, page 2 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part A: Context, focus, and community</strong> <span>Page 2 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S1_A_ContextEdFocusCommunityPrac.docx\" download>Part A Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"B\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part B: Planning agenda, page 1 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part B: Planning agenda, page 1 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-B-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part B: Planning agenda, page 1 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part B: Planning agenda</strong> <span>Page 1 of 2 / Optional format</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_B_PlanningMeetingAgenda_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Part B Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"B\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part B: Planning agenda, page 2 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part B: Planning agenda, page 2 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-B-2.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part B: Planning agenda, page 2 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part B: Planning agenda</strong> <span>Page 2 of 2 / Optional format</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_B_PlanningMeetingAgenda_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Part B Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"C\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part C: Planning minutes, page 1 of 1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part C: Planning minutes, page 1 of 1\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-C-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part C: Planning minutes, page 1 of 1. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part C: Planning minutes</strong> <span>Page 1 of 1 / Optional format</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_C_PlanningMeetingMinutes_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Part C Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"D\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part D: Problem and strategy, page 1 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part D: Problem and strategy, page 1 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-D-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part D: Problem and strategy, page 1 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part D: Problem and strategy</strong> <span>Page 1 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_D_ProbPracDescStrategy.docx\" download>Part D Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"D\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part D: Problem and strategy, page 2 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part D: Problem and strategy, page 2 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-D-2.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part D: Problem and strategy, page 2 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part D: Problem and strategy</strong> <span>Page 2 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_D_ProbPracDescStrategy.docx\" download>Part D Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"E\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part E: Implementation agendas, page 1 of 3\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part E: Implementation agendas, page 1 of 3\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-E-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part E: Implementation agendas, page 1 of 3. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part E: Implementation agendas</strong> <span>Page 1 of 3 / Optional format</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_E_AgendasImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Part E Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"E\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part E: Implementation agendas, page 2 of 3\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part E: Implementation agendas, page 2 of 3\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-E-2.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part E: Implementation agendas, page 2 of 3. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part E: Implementation agendas</strong> <span>Page 2 of 3 / Optional format</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_E_AgendasImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Part E Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"E\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part E: Implementation agendas, page 3 of 3\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part E: Implementation agendas, page 3 of 3\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-E-3.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part E: Implementation agendas, page 3 of 3. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part E: Implementation agendas</strong> <span>Page 3 of 3 / Optional format</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_E_AgendasImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Part E Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"F\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part F: Implementation minutes, page 1 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part F: Implementation minutes, page 1 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-F-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part F: Implementation minutes, page 1 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part F: Implementation minutes</strong> <span>Page 1 of 2 / Optional format</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_F_MinutesImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Part F Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"F\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part F: Implementation minutes, page 2 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part F: Implementation minutes, page 2 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-F-2.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part F: Implementation minutes, page 2 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part F: Implementation minutes</strong> <span>Page 2 of 2 / Optional format</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_F_MinutesImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Part F Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"I\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part I: Video commentary, page 1 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part I: Video commentary, page 1 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-I-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part I: Video commentary, page 1 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part I: Video commentary</strong> <span>Page 1 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_I_CommentaryImplementationMeetings.docx\" download>Part I Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"I\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part I: Video commentary, page 2 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part I: Video commentary, page 2 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-I-2.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part I: Video commentary, page 2 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part I: Video commentary</strong> <span>Page 2 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_I_CommentaryImplementationMeetings.docx\" download>Part I Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"J\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 2, Part J: Reflection, page 1 of 1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 2, Part J: Reflection, page 1 of 1\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-J-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 2, Part J: Reflection, page 1 of 1. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part J: Reflection</strong> <span>Page 1 of 1</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Part J Word template</a></figure></div><p class=\"source-note\">Parts G, H have no separate blank template in the supplied current materials. Their evidence requirements are explained below and in the handbook.</p><p class=\"source-note\">The meeting-record formats for Parts B, C, E, and F are optional; the required agendas and minutes are not.</p></section><section class=\"ov-preparation\"><h3>Before you begin</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Preparation guidance for the required work, not additional submission items. Possible people and records are suggestions; select those relevant to your setting.</p><dl><div><dt>Materials and access</dt><dd>Gather records of collaborative professional learning, school goals, and relevant student learning or well-being data. Arrange access to evidence the group can revisit during implementation, such as student work or agreed measures.</dd></div><div><dt>People to involve</dt><dd>Consult an administrator or teacher leader about the educational focus. Establish a community with three to five educational partners in addition to you. Explain members&#x27; responsibilities, perspectives, demographic context, and your working relationship with each person.</dd></div><div><dt>Conversations to have</dt><dd>First investigate existing professional learning. In planning, invite members to interpret data and define the problem and strategy together. During implementation, ask what was tried, what students experienced, what the evidence suggests, and what to adjust. Seek feedback on your facilitation.</dd></div><div><dt>Schedule and permissions</dt><dd>Arrange the planning meeting and at least three implementation meetings, allowing time for actual use of the strategy between discussions. Obtain required recording permissions and test sound and framing before meetings. Select authentic moments after recording, not staged exchanges.</dd></div><div><dt>Evidence to preserve</dt><dd>Keep agendas, minutes, collaborative work products, and dated implementation evidence connected to decisions. Part H requires three clips, each up to five minutes. Commentary explains your actions and reasoning; it does not replace the meeting evidence. Optional agenda/minutes formats do not make the records optional.</dd></div></dl></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c2-step1\"><h3>Step 1: Investigate</h3><p>Learn how educators currently learn together, agree on a data-supported educational focus with an administrator or teacher leader, and select a purposeful community of practice. The focus comes first; the group develops the specific problem and strategy together in the next step.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubrics: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-2-1\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-2-1\">2.1</a>, <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-2-2\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-2-2\">2.2</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c2-A\" data-part=\"A\"><h4>Part A: Context, focus, and community</h4><p>Investigate current professional learning before explaining the educational focus and membership. A community of practice is sustained shared inquiry, not simply a meeting roster.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S1_A_ContextEdFocusCommunityPrac.docx\" download>Current Part A template</a></p><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">I. Professional learning</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-A-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. How does collaborative professional learning currently work at this school?</h6><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> What formal training was offered during the past three years?</li><li><strong>b.</strong> What professional learning occurs, and how does it affect students?</li><li><strong>c.</strong> What communities of practice exist, and what is their impact?</li><li><strong>d.</strong> What adult-learning principles or research help explain these practices?</li></ul><p>Answer all four subparts: training offered within three years; current professional learning and its impact; communities of practice and their impact; and the adult-learning principles that explain those practices. A community of practice involves sustained shared inquiry, not simply attendance at a meeting.</p><p>Separate what you observed or found in records from what you expect collaboration might accomplish. When impact evidence is unavailable, explain that limitation rather than presenting a benefit as an established result.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-A-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. What does this context mean for how you will facilitate the community?</h6><p>Explain what the investigation requires you to do as facilitator. Connect each priority to the school context, available student evidence, and this group&#x27;s likely participation. Do not repeat the entire description from question 1.</p></section><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">II. Educational focus and membership</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-A-item-3\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. What educational focus do the data and school goals support?</h6><p>Name the educational focus agreed with an administrator or teacher leader, explain the student data supporting it, and connect it to an actual school commitment. The broad focus is established here; the community jointly defines the more specific problem of practice later.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-A-item-4\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. Who will participate, and why is each member included?</h6><p>Explain each partner&#x27;s job title, relevant expertise or responsibility, and reason for inclusion in this educational focus. The community requires three to five partners in addition to you. Shared availability alone is not a membership rationale.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-A-item-5\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How do members&#x27; demographics compare with the school community?</h6><p>Describe each member&#x27;s appropriately provided demographic information and compare it with the school community. Explain representation without assuming that shared identity guarantees shared perspectives or that a person speaks for an entire group.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-A-item-6\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. What is your working relationship with each member?</h6><p>Explain your working relationship and authority in relation to each partner. Consider how grade-level relationships, status, experience, or prior collaboration may shape participation even when you are not an evaluator.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-A-item-7\" data-question=\"5.\"><h6>5. How could this community&#x27;s work benefit students?</h6><p>Explain the pathway from the community&#x27;s adult learning to improved conditions for students. Identify what educators may learn or change, what students could experience differently, and why that could address the selected focus. These are expected effects, not completed outcomes.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-2-1\"><h5>Rubric 2.1: Professional learning context</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.1 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Missing description or analysis can leave the purpose unexplained.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Listing activities is not the same as interpreting their role or impact.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Describe and analyze the connection to student learning using the setting&#x27;s evidence.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Compare different forms and the evidence of their impacts.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Use adult-learning research to critique practice and explain implications.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 2.1</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate describe and analyze the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting and how do these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.1 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not describe the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. OR Candidate does not analyze how these practices relate to student learning and/or well- being.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate minimally describes the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. Candidate provides a vague analysis of how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate provides a clear detailed description of the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. Candidate provides a clear analysis of how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains the relationship between different forms of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting and how these practices have impacted student learning and/or well-being, providing evidence from observations, documentation, surveys, student work, and/or staff consultations.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate critiques collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting, referring to evidence-based adult learning processes and/or research that supports or refutes the practices and outlines implications for facilitating a community of practice at their school site/educational setting.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Written Narrative: Context, Area of Educational Focus, Community of Practice (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 11</a>.</p></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-2-2\"><h5>Rubric 2.2: Focus and membership</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.2 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Missing focus evidence or membership reasoning weakens the foundation.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Minimal detail leaves the fit of the focus or partners unclear.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Explain the focus and each member&#x27;s relevant context, role, and relationship.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Explain how the group&#x27;s work may improve student conditions.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Show intentional attention to diverse perspectives and student learning or well-being.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 2.2</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate select an area of educational focus based on student data that corresponds to the vision, mission, and/or goals of their school site/educational setting? How does the candidate choose a group of educators/educational partners to participate in a community of practice in relation to the selected area of educational focus?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.2 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not select or selects an area of educational focus that is not based on student data or the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate\u2019s school site/educational setting. OR Candidate provides little detail about demographic characteristics and/or does not provide reasons for member inclusion in the community of practice.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate selects and minimally describes an area of educational focus loosely based on student data and/or the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate\u2019s school site/educational setting. Candidate lists group members and provides unclear or irrelevant details of their demographics and characteristics; rationale for member inclusion in the community of practice is not clear.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate selects an area of educational focus, clearly referring to data on student learning and/or well-being, and explains the connection to the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate\u2019s school site/educational setting. Candidate clearly describes community of practice group members by demographic characteristics, current job titles, assignments, and positional relationships and provides clear reasons for each member\u2019s inclusion in the group in relation to the selected area of educational focus.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate articulates how the work of the group is likely to advance conditions for student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides a rationale for including the specific members in their community of practice to ensure diverse viewpoints are represented and respected, leading to supports for all students to learn and to experience a positive sense of well-being.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Written Narrative: Context, Area of Educational Focus, Community of Practice (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1C CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 12</a>.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c2-step2\"><h3>Step 2: Plan</h3><p>Facilitate a planning meeting in which members interpret evidence, define a problem of practice, and choose one evidence-based strategy. Plan how members will learn the strategy, use it, and examine its effects. A problem of practice identifies something in current professional practice that can change.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubrics: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-2-3\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-2-3\">2.3</a>, <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-2-4\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-2-4\">2.4</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c2-B\" data-part=\"B\"><h4>Part B: Planning agenda</h4><p>Prepare the meeting before it occurs. Include who will attend, the purpose, norms, data, discussion of the problem, strategy selection, implementation planning, and next actions. The prompts below are the current optional format; the agenda itself is required.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_B_PlanningMeetingAgenda_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Current Part B template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-B-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. Which school commitments will the group consider?</h6><p>Plan to examine the vision, mission, or goals that make this focus relevant.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-B-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. Which data will the group examine and discuss?</h6><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> Which data sources will you bring?</li><li><strong>b.</strong> What will members discuss about the data?</li></ul><p>Identify the sources and plan time for members to interpret the findings, question definitions, and compare experiences.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-B-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How will the group identify a problem of practice?</h6><p>Plan a discussion that moves from student evidence to a specific, changeable aspect of professional practice. Do not prewrite the group&#x27;s decision.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-B-item-4\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. How will the group consider an evidence-based strategy?</h6><p>Arrange a discussion of relevant approaches and their evidence so members can select one strategy together.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-B-item-5\" data-question=\"5.\"><h6>5. How will the group plan implementation?</h6><p>Discuss how members will learn and use the strategy, responsibilities, timing, support, and evidence to collect.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-B-item-6\" data-question=\"6.\"><h6>6. How will the meeting close with next actions?</h6><p>Reserve time to confirm agreements, responsibilities, and what participants will bring to the next meeting.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c2-C\" data-part=\"C\"><h4>Part C: Planning minutes</h4><p>Record the actual discussion, differing interpretations, agreements, and next steps. Show how the group moved from evidence to a changeable problem and a selected strategy. Minutes are a record of the group&#x27;s reasoning, not a second agenda or a transcript of everything said.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_C_PlanningMeetingMinutes_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Current Part C template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-C-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. Which school commitments did the group discuss?</h6><p>Record the actual commitments considered and how they informed the discussion.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-C-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. What did the group learn from the data?</h6><p>Record interpretations and important differences of view, not just the names of datasets.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-C-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. What problem of practice did the group identify?</h6><p>State the shared problem and the evidence and reasoning that led to it.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-C-item-4\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. Which strategy did the group select, and why?</h6><p>Record the strategy, rationale, and how members contributed to the decision.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-C-item-5\" data-question=\"5.\"><h6>5. What implementation arrangements did the group agree on?</h6><p>Document responsibilities, preparation, timing, support, and the evidence members will collect.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-C-item-6\" data-question=\"6.\"><h6>6. What next steps and action items were agreed?</h6><p>Record who will do what and when. These are the actual agreements, not the agenda&#x27;s anticipated outcomes.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c2-D\" data-part=\"D\"><h4>Part D: Problem and strategy</h4><p>Explain the group&#x27;s reasoning about the problem and strategy. The current template labels these sections II and III; the handbook labels the corresponding planning sections I and II. Follow the template numbering and match the tasks by their titles.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_D_ProbPracDescStrategy.docx\" download>Current Part D template</a></p><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">II. Problem of practice</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-D-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. How did the group analyze data and select the problem together?</h6><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> How did you work together to analyze the student data?</li><li><strong>b.</strong> How did the group select a problem within the educational focus?</li><li><strong>c.</strong> How could changing current practice improve student learning or well-being?</li></ul><p>Explain the collaborative reasoning: which data the group examined, what members noticed, which problem they selected, and how changing current instruction could help. Keep the educational focus, problem of practice, and selected strategy distinct.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-D-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. How did the group examine differences among student groups?</h6><p>Explain how the group compared data across student groups. Identify the measures, populations, and findings, then explain what members made of the differences. Mentioning several group names does not demonstrate a differentiated analysis.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-D-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How did you help the group identify a change in current practice?</h6><p>Explain how you helped the group recognize that something adults currently do can change. Name the instructional practice under discussion and show how members connected that change to the evidence. This is separate from merely identifying low performance.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-D-item-4\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. How does the problem connect to school goals, standards, and student-group differences?</h6><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> How does the problem connect to school vision, mission, or goals?</li><li><strong>b.</strong> How does it relate to relevant standards or curriculum frameworks?</li><li><strong>c.</strong> How does it address differences in student-group performance or well-being?</li></ul><p>Address all three connections: school commitments, relevant standards or frameworks, and actual differences in student learning or well-being. Explain how each connection supports the selected problem, not just that the problem aligns with them.</p></section><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">III. Strategy</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-D-item-5\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. How did the group jointly choose one evidence-based strategy?</h6><p>Explain how the group considered and selected one relevant evidence-based strategy. Include the alternatives, criteria, member contributions, and your facilitation. The group&#x27;s decision is the object of analysis, not just your preferred strategy.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-D-item-6\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. Why this strategy, how will members learn it, and what challenges need attention?</h6><p>Explain why the group chose the strategy, how members will learn to use it, and how anticipated challenges will be addressed. Familiarity with a general approach does not replace a plan for learning and implementing the selected strategy.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-D-item-7\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How is the strategy expected to benefit students?</h6><p>Describe what different students are expected to learn or experience because of the selected strategy. Explain the instructional mechanism: what teachers will do differently and why that should address the identified need. Keep predictions distinct from outcomes.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-D-item-8\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. How will the group monitor implementation and examine results?</h6><p>Explain how the group will check both whether the strategy was implemented and what students initially learned. Specify evidence, timing, interpretation, and decision rules. The monitoring process must be manageable and jointly understood.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-2-3\"><h5>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.3 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>A problem selected without the group or evidence is not collaborative inquiry.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Participation or data use may be too limited to establish shared reasoning.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Explain shared analysis, the selected problem, and the practice that can change.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Differentiate needs across more than one student group with evidence.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Analyze how and why your facilitation advanced the group&#x27;s reasoning.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 2.3</summary><p>Essential Question: Using the agreed-upon area of educational focus as a starting point, how does the candidate work collaboratively with the group to review data sets and select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being? How does the candidate work with the group members to acknowledge that the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current instructional practice at their school site/educational setting?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.3 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not involve the group in analyzing data sets to select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. OR Candidate provides no evidence that they worked collaboratively with the group to select the problem of practice.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate minimally involves the group in the analysis of data sets and/or the selection of the problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. Candidate minimally involves the group in the selection of the problem of practice and/or minimally describes how the problem of practice might be mitigated by a change of current practices at their school site/educational setting.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate clearly explains how they worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the selected educational focus and select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. Candidate clearly describes how they worked collaboratively with group members to acknowledge that the problem of practice might be mitigated by a change of current practices at their school site/educational setting.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate differentiates the academic performance for more than one student group and/or issue of well-being, providing supporting student data/evidence and explains how they facilitated the group to collaboratively address these differences in defining the problem of practice.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains in detail both how they facilitated the collaborative process with the group members to analyze student data/evidence and why specific facilitation skills were used.</p></section></div><p>Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Planning Meeting Agenda (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part C: Planning Meeting Minutes (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part D: Written Narrative: Identifying a Problem of Practice and a Strategy to Address It (up to 4 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1C CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5B, 5C</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 16</a>.</p></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-2-4\"><h5>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.4 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>A strategy without joint selection or a relevant rationale is insufficient.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>A vague choice leaves the expected benefit unexplained.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Explain the process, evidence base, and potential effect.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Specify how different student groups may benefit.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Anticipate implementation challenges across groups and feasible responses.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 2.4</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy to address the selected problem of practice? How does the candidate describe the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.4 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence- based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate does not describe the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate vaguely explains the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence- based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate minimally discusses the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate clearly explains the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence- based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate clearly describes the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate describes in detail how the collaboratively determined evidence-based strategy will have a positive impact on student learning and/or well-being across specific student groups.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate thoroughly explains potential implementation implications and/or challenges across multiple student groups and suggests feasible next steps for each group, as appropriate.</p></section></div><p>Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Planning Meeting Agenda (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part C: Planning Meeting Minutes (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part D: Written Narrative: Identifying a Problem of Practice and a Strategy to Address It (up to 4 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 17</a>.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c2-step3\"><h3>Step 3: Act</h3><p>Support actual implementation while facilitating at least three implementation meetings, separate from the planning meeting. Help members examine initial evidence, consider different interpretations, and agree on next steps. Agendas, minutes, products, clips, and commentary each document a different aspect of this work.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubrics: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-2-5\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-2-5\">2.5</a>, <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-2-6\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-2-6\">2.6</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c2-E\" data-part=\"E\"><h4>Part E: Implementation agendas</h4><p>Prepare an agenda for each of the three implementation meetings, including meeting information, norms, and the prompts below. The same numbered items recur, but the evidence should develop as the strategy is used. The format is optional; the meeting records are required.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_E_AgendasImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Current Part E template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-E-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. What early results or new data will the group examine?</h6><p>Plan discussion of evidence from actual use of the strategy. Revisit previous decisions and identify what the new evidence may clarify.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-E-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. How will the group summarize what it learned?</h6><p>Reserve time to synthesize findings and unresolved questions. Do not write the group&#x27;s conclusions before the meeting occurs.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-E-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How will the group decide what to do next?</h6><p>Plan time for joint decisions about continuation, adjustment, support, and evidence for the next discussion.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c2-F\" data-part=\"F\"><h4>Part F: Implementation minutes</h4><p>Record what actually happened in each of the three implementation meetings. Include the meeting information, member contributions, evidence considered, decisions, and agreed follow-through. The lettered items below are not new numbered questions.</p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-F-item-1\" data-question=\"a.\"><h6>a. What is going well?</h6><p>Record what members report is working and the evidence supporting that interpretation. Separate a positive impression from an observed change.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-F-item-2\" data-question=\"b.\"><h6>b. What needs adjustment?</h6><p>Record the concern, the evidence behind it, and the change or support the group proposes.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-F-item-3\" data-question=\"c.\"><h6>c. What are participants learning?</h6><p>Document member reflections, differing views, and lessons from using the strategy, not only your own evaluation of the meeting.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-F-item-4\" data-question=\"\"><h6> What did the group conclude and agree to do next?</h6><p>Summarize actual agreements, remaining questions, responsibilities, and follow-through. Preserve these records for each of the three implementation meetings.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c2-G\" data-part=\"G\"><h4>Part G: Collaborative work products</h4><p>Provide the actual collaborative products used or created during the inquiry. Select material that makes the group&#x27;s work understandable and observe the handbook&#x27;s submission limit.</p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-G-item-1\" data-question=\"\"><h6> Which work products document the group&#x27;s work?</h6><p>Submit actual products used or created in the community&#x27;s work, within the handbook&#x27;s page limit. Explain their purpose, when the group used them, and which decisions they support. A product can document planning, implementation, or a result, but those functions should not be confused.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c2-H\" data-part=\"H\"><h4>Part H: Implementation video clips</h4><p>Use recordings of genuine implementation meetings. These are evidence of your facilitation, not a presentation in which you describe meetings afterward.</p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-H-item-1\" data-question=\"Clip 1\"><h6>Clip 1 How did you establish purpose and examine data?</h6><p>Select authentic implementation-meeting evidence of establishing purpose and reviewing the relevant data. Align the commentary to the actions actually visible and audible.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-H-item-2\" data-question=\"Clip 2\"><h6>Clip 2 How did you facilitate professional collaboration?</h6><p>Select evidence of members reasoning together and of your facilitation of participation, different viewpoints, and shared decisions.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-H-item-3\" data-question=\"Clip 3\"><h6>Clip 3 How did the group interpret results and agree on next steps?</h6><p>Select evidence of a shared interpretation of initial results and jointly determined next actions. Submit exactly three clips, each up to five minutes.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c2-I\" data-part=\"I\"><h4>Part I: Video commentary</h4><p>Address all five prompts at least once across the three clips. Use clip identifiers and timestamps to connect what you did, why you did it, and its effect to the actual recording.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_I_CommentaryImplementationMeetings.docx\" download>Current Part I template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-I-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. How did you establish the meeting&#x27;s purpose?</h6><p>Use a specific clip moment to explain how you established the meeting&#x27;s purpose and connected it to the problem of practice. Include what you did, why, and the response or change in the discussion that supports your interpretation of its impact.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-I-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. How did you help the group evaluate implementation evidence?</h6><p>Explain how the group interpreted evidence from using the strategy. Distinguish baseline information, implementation observations, and post-assessment findings. Show your role in helping members examine what the evidence does and does not establish.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-I-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How did you support agreement and shared understanding?</h6><p>Describe the agreement and how members arrived at it. Identify the evidence, alternatives or concerns, and your action in checking understanding. Agreement is not established solely because the facilitator summarized a conclusion.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-I-item-4\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. How did you support different viewpoints, feedback, focus, and progress?</h6><p>Address all four components: diverse viewpoints, individual and collective feedback, group focus and energy, and progress toward the meeting goals. Analyze the effect of your actions on participation and reasoning, not simply your use of polite language.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-I-item-5\" data-question=\"5.\"><h6>5. How did the group decide on next steps together?</h6><p>Show how evidence led the group to agree on its next actions. Explain what the candidate and members will do, how the plan responds to different needs, and how later evidence will reveal whether the response was useful.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-2-5\"><h5>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.5 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>A summary of a meeting is not an analysis of demonstrated facilitation.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Minimal evidence or vague commentary leaves actions and effects unclear.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Demonstrate consensus building, documented decisions, diverse viewpoints, and group focus; analyze your facilitation.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Intentionally invite contributions from every member.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Use relevant research to critique your own facilitation.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 2.5</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze a variety of their facilitation skills of group learning\u2014 including documenting group decisions, supporting diverse viewpoints, and/or maintaining group focus and energy\u2014 while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus, feedback, and progress during the implementation meetings?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.5 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not demonstrate facilitation skills during the implementation meetings. OR Candidate\u2019s commentary does not provide an analysis of their facilitation of group learning or is a summary of what was observable in the video.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate demonstrates minimal facilitation skills that are vaguely supportive of group learning during the implementation meetings while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus documenting group decisions, respecting diverse viewpoints, and/or maintaining group focus and energy. Candidate\u2019s commentary does not clearly provide an analysis (what they did and why) of their facilitation of group learning.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate clearly demonstrates facilitation skills that support group learning during the implementation meetings while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus, including documenting group decisions, respecting diverse viewpoints, and maintaining group focus and energy. Candidate\u2019s commentary provides a clear analysis (what they did and why) of their facilitation of group learning.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate purposefully seeks diverse viewpoints and clearly encourages all members of the community to speak and share ideas during meetings.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate uses their commentary to critique their facilitation skills used during the implementation meetings based on group learning processes and/or research that supports facilitation practices.</p></section></div><p>Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part E: Agendas for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part F: Minutes for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part G: Key Collaborative Work Products (up to 10 pages) \u2022 Part H: 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (up to 5 minutes each) \u2022 Part I: Commentary for the 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 23</a>.</p></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-2-6\"><h5>Rubric 2.6: Results and next steps</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.6 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Missing results, joint next steps, or analysis leaves the inquiry incomplete.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Unclear consensus or one-sided decisions weaken the account.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Facilitate shared interpretation of initial results and joint next steps; analyze your contribution.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Show follow-through within and between meetings.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Show joint adaptation in response to successes and challenges.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 2.6</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate facilitate a discussion that leads to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results? How does the candidate collaboratively lead the group in jointly determining next steps?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.6 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not facilitate a discussion about implementation results. OR Next steps are not determined. OR Candidate\u2019s commentary does not provide an analysis of their facilitation of group discussion or is a summary of what was observable in the video.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate facilitates a discussion that does not clearly lead to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results. It is not clear that the candidate works collaboratively with the group to jointly determine the next steps. Candidate\u2019s commentary provides a vague analysis of their facilitation of group discussions.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate facilitates a discussion that clearly leads to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results. Candidate clearly works collaboratively with the group to jointly determine next steps. Candidate\u2019s commentary provides a clear analysis of their facilitation of the group discussions.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate consistently addresses initial results and next steps to inform the collaborative implementation process with group members both within and between meetings.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate collaborates with the group to adjust the implementation strategy as needed in response to its challenges or successes.</p></section></div><p>Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part E: Agendas for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part F: Minutes for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part G: Key Collaborative Work Products (up to 10 pages) \u2022 Part H: 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (up to 5 minutes each) \u2022 Part I: Commentary for the 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1B CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2C</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 24</a>.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c2-step4\"><h3>Step 4: Reflect</h3><p>Use initial implementation results and member feedback to analyze your facilitation. Examine participation, integrity, equity, contextual constraints, and your next professional learning needs. Completing meetings is not itself evidence of effective leadership.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubric: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-2-7\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-2-7\">2.7</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c2-J\" data-part=\"J\"><h4>Part J: Reflection</h4><p>Bring initial results, member feedback, and specific facilitation decisions together. Reflect on the limits as well as the accomplishments of this inquiry.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Current Part J template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-J-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. What do results and feedback reveal about your leadership and growth?</h6><p>Bring the evidence, your leadership actions, initial results, member feedback, strengths, growth needs, equitable facilitation, and professional next steps into one analysis. The question asks what the evidence teaches you about your leadership, not only whether the group completed its work.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-J-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. How did you demonstrate professionalism, integrity, and equity?</h6><p>Analyze how you acted professionally and equitably throughout the inquiry. Use decisions or difficult moments to show how you treated disagreement, represented evidence accurately, and shared influence. Listing norms is not the same as demonstrating their use.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-J-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How did school context affect the work, and how did you respond?</h6><p>Explain which features of school culture or scheduling supported or constrained the work, what you did in response, and what resulted. Distinguish the group&#x27;s independent work from meetings you personally facilitated.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c2-J-item-4\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. What professional learning goals follow from this cycle?</h6><p>Set specific professional learning goals derived from the cycle&#x27;s evidence. Explain what you will practice or study, where you will apply it, what feedback you will seek, and how you will judge progress.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-2-7\"><h5>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on facilitation</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.7 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Unsupported reflection, missing results or feedback, or no equity connection creates gaps.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Vague references do not show how evidence supports the judgment.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Use initial results, member feedback, and specific evidence to analyze equitable facilitation, strengths, growth, and professional next steps.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity across the inquiry.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Use relevant research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 2.7</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate reflect upon initial implementation results and feedback from the group to analyze their leadership skills and practices to make specific connections to their strengths and areas for growth, and identify next steps, referring to specific evidence from any of the four steps? How does the candidate demonstrate a variety of leadership skills used to equitably facilitate a community of practice?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"2.7 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate provides no connection to initial implementation results and/or feedback from the group to make specific connections to their strengths or areas for growth, or next steps related to leadership practices. OR Candidate does not include specific evidence from any of the cycle\u2019s four steps to support their reflections and inform their analysis. OR Candidate does not describe their leadership practice using an equitable leadership approach to facilitate a community of practice.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate vaguely or inaccurately reflects upon initial implementation results and/or feedback from the group to make connections to their strengths, areas for growth, and/or next steps. Candidate vaguely refers to evidence from any of the cycle\u2019s four steps to support their reflections and/or inform their analysis. Candidate vaguely demonstrates their capacity to facilitate a community of practice. It is not clear that the candidate has an equitable approach to leadership practice.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate clearly reflects upon initial implementation results and feedback from the group to analyze their leadership skills, making specific connections to their strengths, areas for growth, and next steps. Candidate clearly refers to specific, appropriate evidence from any of the four steps to support their reflections and inform their analysis. Candidate clearly demonstrates how their leadership practice equitably facilitated a community of practice.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides a deep analysis that illustrates how they maintained professionalism and integrity as well as employed an equitable leadership approach consistently throughout the community of practice inquiry process.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate consults evidence- based practices and/or research related to equitable leadership development, adult learning, and/or group facilitation, then uses the information to analyze their own leadership practices and set professional goals during this cycle.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 5 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5B</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 27</a>.</p></section></section><p><a href=\"#cycle/c2\">Continue to the detailed Cycle 2 guide and examples</a></p>", "c3": "<div class=\"ov-map\"><table class=\"ov-question-map\"><caption class=\"sr-only\">Cycle 3 parts and questions by step</caption><thead><tr><th id=\"ov-c3-step1-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 1</span>Investigate</th><th id=\"ov-c3-step2-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 2</span>Plan</th><th id=\"ov-c3-step3-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 3</span>Act</th><th id=\"ov-c3-step4-head\" scope=\"col\"><span>Step 4</span>Reflect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td headers=\"ov-c3-step1-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 1: Investigate</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c3-A\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-A\">Part A</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">School practices and teacher context</p><h5>I. Current school practices</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-A-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-A-item-1\">Analyze frequency, goals, and effectiveness</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-A-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-A-item-2\">Explain processes and documents</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-A-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-A-item-3\">Explain teacher input</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-A-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-A-item-4\">Identify implications for this cycle</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>5.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-A-item-5\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-A-item-5\">Explain the CSTPs&#x27; role in teacher growth</a></p><h5>II. Volunteer teacher and coaching context</h5><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-A-item-6\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-A-item-6\">Describe the volunteer teacher</a></p><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> Professional experience and languages</li><li><strong>b.</strong> Current assignment</li><li><strong>c.</strong> Previous coaching experiences</li><li><strong>d.</strong> Knowledge and use of CSTPs</li></ul><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-A-item-7\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-A-item-7\">Explain the context-informed coaching plan</a></p><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> Orientation</li><li><strong>b.</strong> Pre-observation meeting</li><li><strong>c.</strong> Observation and evidence collection</li><li><strong>d.</strong> Joint post-observation meeting</li></ul><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-A-item-8\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-A-item-8\">Anticipate challenges and responses</a></p></section></td><td headers=\"ov-c3-step2-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 2: Plan</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c3-B\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-B\">Part B</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Classroom context</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-B-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-B-item-1\">Describe the lesson context</a></p><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> Goals, prior knowledge, and prior lesson</li><li><strong>b.</strong> Setting and organization</li><li><strong>c.</strong> Student assets and learning needs</li><li><strong>d.</strong> Materials and resources</li><li><strong>e.</strong> Other relevant classroom context</li></ul></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c3-C\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-C\">Part C</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Teacher lesson plan</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong></strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-C-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-C-item-1\">Provide the plan and student-work description</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c3-D\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-D\">Part D</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Pre-observation video clips</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>Clip 1</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-D-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-D-item-1\">Teacher needs, lesson review, and CSTP selection</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>Clip 2</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-D-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-D-item-2\">Observation process and evidence</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c3-E\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-E\">Part E</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Pre-observation commentary</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-E-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-E-item-1\">Support teacher self-assessment</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-E-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-E-item-2\">Review the lesson together</a></p><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> Student work product</li><li><strong>b.</strong> Classroom context</li><li><strong>c.</strong> Student assets and learning needs</li></ul><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-E-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-E-item-3\">Select the CSTP focus jointly</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>4.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-E-item-4\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-E-item-4\">Agree on process and observation evidence</a></p></section></td><td headers=\"ov-c3-step3-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 3: Act</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c3-F\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-F\">Part F</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Observation notes</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong></strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-F-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-F-item-1\">Document specific CSTP-focused evidence</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c3-G\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-G\">Part G</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Student work</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong></strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-G-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-G-item-1\">Provide lesson work used in the joint review</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c3-H\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-H\">Part H</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Post-observation video clips</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong></strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-H-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-H-item-1\">Submit 1-3 clips of the joint review</a></p></section><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c3-I\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-I\">Part I</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Post-observation commentary</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-I-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-I-item-1\">Support assessment of learning; share and co-analyze evidence</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-I-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-I-item-2\">Discuss teaching effectiveness and co-determine next steps</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-I-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-I-item-3\">Interpret teacher feedback on your coaching</a></p></section></td><td headers=\"ov-c3-step4-head\"><div class=\"ov-mobile-step\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Step 4: Reflect</div><section class=\"ov-map-part\"><h4><a href=\"#home/ov-c3-J\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-J\">Part J</a></h4><p class=\"ov-part-title\">Reflection</p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>1.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-J-item-1\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-J-item-1\">Analyze coaching strengths and growth using feedback</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>2.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-J-item-2\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-J-item-2\">Explain two-way conversation and teacher ownership</a></p><p class=\"ov-map-question\"><strong>3.</strong> <a href=\"#home/ov-c3-J-item-3\" data-jump=\"ov-c3-J-item-3\">Reflect on equitable leadership and coaching&#x27;s potential</a></p></section></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note\">Plain-language summaries with current template numbering. Roman numerals identify sections; letters identify subparts. Unnumbered evidence tasks remain unnumbered. The official template and handbook govern.</p><section class=\"ov-templates\" id=\"ov-c3-templates\"><h3>Current blank templates</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original pages from the supplied CTC Version 09 templates, in part and page order.</p><div class=\"ov-template-gallery\"><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"A\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 3, Part A: School practices and teacher context, page 1 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 3, Part A: School practices and teacher context, page 1 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-A-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 3, Part A: School practices and teacher context, page 1 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part A: School practices and teacher context</strong> <span>Page 1 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S1_A_CoachingObsFeedbackPracVolTeacher.docx\" download>Part A Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"A\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 3, Part A: School practices and teacher context, page 2 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 3, Part A: School practices and teacher context, page 2 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-A-2.png\" alt=\"Cycle 3, Part A: School practices and teacher context, page 2 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part A: School practices and teacher context</strong> <span>Page 2 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S1_A_CoachingObsFeedbackPracVolTeacher.docx\" download>Part A Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"B\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 3, Part B: Classroom context, page 1 of 1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 3, Part B: Classroom context, page 1 of 1\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-B-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 3, Part B: Classroom context, page 1 of 1. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part B: Classroom context</strong> <span>Page 1 of 1</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_B_ClassroomContextLessonObservation.docx\" download>Part B Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"E\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 3, Part E: Pre-observation commentary, page 1 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 3, Part E: Pre-observation commentary, page 1 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-E-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 3, Part E: Pre-observation commentary, page 1 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part E: Pre-observation commentary</strong> <span>Page 1 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_E_CommentaryPreObservationMeeting.docx\" download>Part E Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"E\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 3, Part E: Pre-observation commentary, page 2 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 3, Part E: Pre-observation commentary, page 2 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-E-2.png\" alt=\"Cycle 3, Part E: Pre-observation commentary, page 2 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part E: Pre-observation commentary</strong> <span>Page 2 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_E_CommentaryPreObservationMeeting.docx\" download>Part E Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"I\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 3, Part I: Post-observation commentary, page 1 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 3, Part I: Post-observation commentary, page 1 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-I-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 3, Part I: Post-observation commentary, page 1 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part I: Post-observation commentary</strong> <span>Page 1 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S3_I_CommentaryPostObservationMeeting.docx\" download>Part I Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"I\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 3, Part I: Post-observation commentary, page 2 of 2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 3, Part I: Post-observation commentary, page 2 of 2\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-I-2.png\" alt=\"Cycle 3, Part I: Post-observation commentary, page 2 of 2. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part I: Post-observation commentary</strong> <span>Page 2 of 2</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S3_I_CommentaryPostObservationMeeting.docx\" download>Part I Word template</a></figure><figure class=\"ov-template-page\" data-template-part=\"J\"><button class=\"ov-template-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Cycle 3, Part J: Reflection, page 1 of 1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Cycle 3, Part J: Reflection, page 1 of 1\" title=\"Enlarge original template page\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-J-1.png\" alt=\"Cycle 3, Part J: Reflection, page 1 of 1. Original blank CTC Version 09 template.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption><strong>Part J: Reflection</strong> <span>Page 1 of 1</span></figcaption><a class=\"ov-template-download\" href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Part J Word template</a></figure></div><p class=\"source-note\">Parts C, D, F, G, H have no separate blank template in the supplied current materials. Their evidence requirements are explained below and in the handbook.</p></section><section class=\"ov-preparation\"><h3>Before you begin</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Preparation guidance for the required work, not additional submission items. Possible people and records are suggestions; select those relevant to your setting.</p><dl><div><dt>Materials and access</dt><dd>Gather school coaching and observation procedures, the relevant CSTP materials, the volunteer teacher&#x27;s lesson plan, classroom context, and the intended student-work task. Plan an observation record tied to the jointly selected focus.</dd></div><div><dt>People to involve</dt><dd>Partner with a volunteer teacher. Learn about the teacher&#x27;s assignment, experience, languages, prior coaching, and professional interests. Coordinate with site personnel for scheduling, access, and required permissions without taking ownership of the teacher&#x27;s goals.</dd></div><div><dt>Conversations to have</dt><dd>Orient the teacher to the process; discuss the lesson and teacher self-assessment before observing; jointly interpret the evidence afterward. Ask what the teacher notices, what students learned, what remains uncertain, and which next steps and support would help. Ask directly for feedback on your coaching.</dd></div><div><dt>Schedule and recording</dt><dd>Arrange orientation, pre-observation meeting, a classroom observation of at least 20 minutes, and post-observation meeting. Record the full lesson observation for joint review. The lesson recording is not the submitted meeting clips. Follow site permission and confidentiality procedures.</dd></div><div><dt>Evidence to preserve</dt><dd>Keep CSTP-focused observation notes and actual student work. Part D requires two pre-observation clips, up to six minutes each. Part H permits one to three post-observation clips, each at least one minute, totaling no more than fifteen minutes. Arrange resources and follow-up support with the teacher.</dd></div></dl></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c3-step1\"><h3>Step 1: Investigate</h3><p>Investigate the school&#x27;s coaching, observation, and feedback practices and learn about the volunteer teacher. Explain how both inform your partnership. Coaching supports teacher learning; describing school evaluation procedures alone does not explain the approach you will take.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubric: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-3-1\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-3-1\">3.1</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c3-A\" data-part=\"A\"><h4>Part A: School practices and teacher context</h4><p>Explain the school&#x27;s practices and the teacher&#x27;s context before setting out the coaching plan. The current template includes I.5 about CSTPs; the handbook discusses this requirement in prose outside its four-item school-context list.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S1_A_CoachingObsFeedbackPracVolTeacher.docx\" download>Current Part A template</a></p><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">I. Current school practices</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-A-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. How often does coaching or observation occur, why, and how effective is it?</h6><p>Explain frequency, procedures, and intended outcomes for the school, teachers, and students, then evaluate effectiveness using available evidence. Distinguish evaluation, walkthroughs, and voluntary instructional coaching; they may serve different purposes and create different relationships.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-A-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. What processes and documents guide observation and feedback?</h6><p>Explain how documents are used before, during, and after observation. Identify what each tool records, how it connects to the CSTP focus, and how teachers use the resulting evidence. Naming a form is not a description of the process.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-A-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How do teachers influence their coaching and observation?</h6><p>Explain which decisions teachers influence, how they exercise that choice, and any limits on it. Distinguish selecting from options offered by an administrator from genuinely shaping the purpose, evidence, and goals of coaching.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-A-item-4\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. What do the school&#x27;s practices mean for your approach in this cycle?</h6><p>Explain what you will retain, adapt, or clarify because of the school&#x27;s practices and this teacher&#x27;s experience. The task asks for implications, not merely a statement that the teacher already knows the process.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-A-item-5\" data-question=\"5.\"><h6>5. How do the CSTPs guide teacher growth at this school?</h6><p>Explain how the standards guide ongoing growth: identifying a need, focusing evidence, interpreting practice, and deciding next steps. If a different local framework is used, provide the required crosswalk for the selected elements.</p></section><h5 class=\"ov-section-label\">II. Volunteer teacher and coaching context</h5><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-A-item-6\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. What should you understand about the volunteer teacher?</h6><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> What professional experience and languages does the teacher bring?</li><li><strong>b.</strong> What is the teacher&#x27;s current assignment?</li><li><strong>c.</strong> What previous coaching or observation experiences has the teacher had?</li><li><strong>d.</strong> How familiar is the teacher with the CSTPs, and how have they used them?</li></ul><p>Address all four subparts: professional experience and languages, current assignment, previous coaching experiences, and knowledge and use of the CSTPs. Explain the background accurately enough to support the individualized plan in the next question.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-A-item-7\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. How will the school and teacher context shape each stage of coaching?</h6><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> How will you orient the teacher to the process?</li><li><strong>b.</strong> How will you conduct the pre-observation meeting?</li><li><strong>c.</strong> How will you observe and collect evidence?</li><li><strong>d.</strong> How will you conduct the joint post-observation meeting?</li></ul><p>Explain how the school and teacher information shapes four connected steps: orientation, pre-observation meeting, observation and evidence collection, and the joint post-observation meeting. State the purpose of each step and the decisions you will make together.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-A-item-8\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. What challenges do you anticipate, and how will you address them?</h6><p>Identify plausible challenges for this teacher and context, explain why each may occur, and give a practical response. Tie the strategy to the challenge rather than listing generic good coaching practices.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-3-1\"><h5>Rubric 3.1: Coaching context</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.1 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Missing purpose or a plan leaves the coaching approach unexplained.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>A list of practices and a vague plan do not show meaningful implications.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Analyze the setting and explain each stage using the teacher&#x27;s experience.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Explain teacher influence and how feedback practices build trust.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Use relevant evidence-based practice or adult-learning theory to explain benefits.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 3.1</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate describe and analyze the current role of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school with the volunteer teacher, and explain the implications of how current practices impact their approach to conducting a coaching cycle?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.1 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not provide an explanation of the purpose or processes for coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school. Candidate outlines a plan for the coaching cycle with little or no details OR does not outline a plan.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate lists the teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school, providing limited details about the processes used. The purpose for conducting these processes is unclear. Candidate outlines a vague plan for the coaching cycle with minimal details about implications of each step of the coaching cycle and/or observation based on the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional experience, current teaching assignment, and past experience in relation to coaching.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate clearly describes and analyzes the current role of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback, discussing the purpose and intended goals of the practices for both teachers and students. Candidate clearly describes a plan for and the implications of each step of the coaching cycle and observation based on the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional experience, current teaching assignment, and past experience in relation to coaching.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate describes how teachers participate in the selection and purpose of the instructional feedback practices at the school to meet the unique needs of the teachers and how the practices build trust between the teachers and school administrators.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate discusses relevant evidence-based practices and/or adult learning theory to explain how school practices of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback support ongoing teacher development and benefit students\u2019 learning and well- being.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Written Narrative: Coaching, Observation, and/or Instructional Feedback Practices at the School and the Volunteer Teacher (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 10</a>.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c3-step2\"><h3>Step 2: Plan</h3><p>Conduct a two-way pre-observation conversation about the lesson, learners, and the teacher&#x27;s self-assessment. Jointly choose one or two CSTP elements within the same domain and agree on the observation process and evidence, including student work.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubric: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-3-2\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-3-2\">3.2</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c3-B\" data-part=\"B\"><h4>Part B: Classroom context</h4><p>Document the context of the actual lesson to be observed. Describe information that affects teaching and learning, not simply a demographic inventory.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_B_ClassroomContextLessonObservation.docx\" download>Current Part B template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-B-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. What classroom and lesson context should inform the coaching?</h6><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> What are the lesson goals, prior knowledge, and connection to the previous lesson?</li><li><strong>b.</strong> How are the setting and learning activities organized?</li><li><strong>c.</strong> What assets and learning needs do students bring?</li><li><strong>d.</strong> What materials and resources will support learning?</li><li><strong>e.</strong> What other classroom circumstances matter for this lesson?</li></ul><p>Address the lesson goals and prior learning, setting, students&#x27; assets and needs, materials, and other relevant context. Explain how this information affects instruction and observation. Distinguish enrollment from attendance in the observed lesson, and engagement from achievement of the learning goals.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c3-C\" data-part=\"C\"><h4>Part C: Teacher lesson plan</h4><p>Submit the teacher&#x27;s lesson plan and description of the student work. This part is an evidence task, not a numbered narrative question.</p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-C-item-1\" data-question=\"\"><h6> What lesson and student work are planned?</h6><p>Provide the volunteer teacher&#x27;s actual plan and a description of the student work that will show learning. Explain how the work relates to the lesson goals. An adopted curriculum plan establishes intended instruction, not proof of what occurred.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c3-D\" data-part=\"D\"><h4>Part D: Pre-observation video clips</h4><p>Select two clips from the authentic pre-observation meeting. They should show a shared planning conversation, not decisions made solely by the coach.</p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-D-item-1\" data-question=\"Clip 1\"><h6>Clip 1 How did the teacher&#x27;s needs and lesson shape the shared CSTP focus?</h6><p>Select evidence of teacher self-assessment, lesson review, and joint selection of one or two CSTP elements in the same domain.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-D-item-2\" data-question=\"Clip 2\"><h6>Clip 2 How did you agree on the observation process and evidence?</h6><p>Select the discussion of observation arrangements and the evidence to collect, including student work. Submit two pre-observation clips, each up to six minutes.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c3-E\" data-part=\"E\"><h4>Part E: Pre-observation commentary</h4><p>Explain your coaching in the pre-observation clips. Use specific moments to analyze listening, questions, teacher thinking, and joint decisions.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_E_CommentaryPreObservationMeeting.docx\" download>Current Part E template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-E-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. How did you support the teacher&#x27;s assessment of instructional needs?</h6><p>Explain how the teacher identified instructional needs and challenges and how your listening and questioning helped connect them to the CSTP focus. Analyze the teacher&#x27;s thinking and the effect of your coaching, not only the questions you planned to ask.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-E-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. How did you and the teacher examine the planned lesson?</h6><ul class=\"ov-subparts\"><li><strong>a.</strong> What student work will show progress toward the learning goals?</li><li><strong>b.</strong> How does the classroom context affect the lesson?</li><li><strong>c.</strong> How does the plan respond to student assets and learning needs?</li></ul><p>Connect the student work, classroom context, and student assets and needs to the lesson review. Show how your questions helped the teacher clarify what students should learn and how the planned evidence will reveal it.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-E-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. How did you jointly select the CSTP focus?</h6><p>Explain why the selected element fits the teacher&#x27;s self-assessment and students&#x27; needs, and how the selection was genuinely joint. The current handbook specifies one or two elements within the same CSTP domain.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-E-item-4\" data-question=\"4.\"><h6>4. How did you agree on the observation process and evidence?</h6><p>Explain the jointly agreed process, duration, focus, and evidence collection. Include teacher practices, student responses, and student work. Analyze how the conversation produced shared understanding rather than presenting logistics as a checklist.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-3-2\"><h5>Rubric 3.2: Planning a coaching partnership</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.2 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>One-sided selection, missing planning, or absent analysis can leave essential evidence missing.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Minimal teacher involvement is not a clearly shared plan.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Demonstrate a two-way lesson discussion, joint CSTP selection, and an evidence plan; analyze your coaching communication.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Use facilitative questions to draw out the teacher&#x27;s specific needs.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Deepen discussion of equitable learning opportunities, redirecting when appropriate.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 3.2</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher to understand the learning goals, classroom context, and student assets (e.g., cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests and learning needs; jointly select with the volunteer teacher one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, including evidence to be collected; and plan for the observation?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.2 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate and the volunteer teacher do not discuss classroom context, learning goals, and/or student assets and/or learning needs. Selection of the CSTP element(s) and/or evidence to be collected, including student work, is made by the candidate without input from the volunteer teacher or element(s) are not selected. OR No plan is established for the coaching and/or observation process. OR In the commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis* of their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate and volunteer teacher broadly discuss classroom context, learning goals, and/or student assets and/or learning needs, including student work. Candidate minimally involves the volunteer teacher in the selection of CSTP element(s) and/or evidence to be collected during the observation. It is not clear what aspects of instruction need to be the focus of the observation, and a plan is not specified and/or agreed to by the volunteer teacher OR candidate creates plan for observation without input from the volunteer teacher. In the commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis* of their ability to listen to and/or talk with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate clearly engages the volunteer teacher in a detailed and focused pre-observation discussion about the classroom context, student assets and learning needs, and content- specific learning goals of the lesson. Candidate and the volunteer teacher jointly select the CSTP element(s) and together determine evidence to be collected during the observation, including student work. Candidate and volunteer teacher create a plan for the observation, clearly identifying the focal aspects of instruction. In the commentary, candidate provides an analysis* of their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate uses facilitative questioning strategies to engage in a two-way discussion with the volunteer teacher to draw out their specific areas of need in relation to learning goals, classroom context, student assets, CSTP element(s), and observation plans.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate uses facilitative questioning strategies, including re-directing as appropriate for the conversation, to deepen discussion regarding equitable opportunities for all students in the class to learn.</p></section></div><p>Note: *Analysis: What the candidate does, why they do it, and the impact their action had Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Written Narrative: Classroom Context, Lesson, and Observation (up to 4 pages) \u2022 Part C: Volunteer Teacher\u2019s Lesson Plan, Including Student Work Product Description \u2022 Part D: 2 Video Clips of the Pre-Observation Meeting (up to 6 minutes each) \u2022 Part E: Commentary of the Pre-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5C</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 15</a>.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c3-step3\"><h3>Step 3: Act</h3><p>Observe the lesson live for at least 20 minutes, record the full observation, and collect focused notes and student work. Then jointly review lesson video and learning evidence in the post-observation meeting. Agree on professional growth steps, resources, and coaching support; obtain feedback on your coaching.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubrics: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-3-3\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-3-3\">3.3</a>, <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-3-4\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-3-4\">3.4</a>, <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-3-5\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-3-5\">3.5</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c3-F\" data-part=\"F\"><h4>Part F: Observation notes</h4><p>Collect notes during the actual lesson observation. Record teacher actions, student responses, and the relationship to the agreed CSTP elements and learning goals.</p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-F-item-1\" data-question=\"\"><h6> What did you observe in relation to the agreed focus?</h6><p>Collect specific evidence during the live observation and connect it to the agreed CSTP element and lesson goals. Separate observed actions and student responses from your interpretation or proposed alternative. Useful notes allow the teacher to examine a claim, not merely accept your judgment.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c3-G\" data-part=\"G\"><h4>Part G: Student work</h4><p>Preserve actual work from the observed lesson for joint analysis with the teacher. Use the work to examine learning, not only participation or task completion.</p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-G-item-1\" data-question=\"\"><h6> What does the actual student work show?</h6><p>Use actual work produced during the lesson and discussed with the teacher. Identify the task, the success criteria, what the work demonstrates, and what remains uncertain. A correct answer, an incorrect answer, and visible reasoning can each provide different information.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c3-H\" data-part=\"H\"><h4>Part H: Post-observation video clips</h4><p>Select post-observation meeting evidence of the teacher and you analyzing the lesson, examining learning, planning growth, and discussing your coaching.</p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-H-item-1\" data-question=\"\"><h6> How did you and the teacher jointly interpret evidence and determine next steps?</h6><p>Submit one to three clips, totaling up to fifteen minutes, from the joint post-observation meeting. The evidence should show examination of the lesson video and student work, discussion of practice, shared next steps, and teacher feedback on your coaching. Each clip must be at least one minute. The lesson recording supports this discussion; these submitted clips document the post-observation meeting.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c3-I\" data-part=\"I\"><h4>Part I: Post-observation commentary</h4><p>Analyze the post-observation coaching using the actual clips, notes, lesson video, student work, and teacher feedback. Identify your contribution to the teacher&#x27;s thinking and the jointly determined plan.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S3_I_CommentaryPostObservationMeeting.docx\" download>Current Part I template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-I-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. How did you support the teacher&#x27;s assessment of learning and jointly analyze evidence?</h6><p>Address all three components: how you supported the teacher&#x27;s assessment of learning, shared CSTP-focused evidence, and jointly analyzed the lesson video and student work. Explain how your coaching helped the teacher interpret evidence, not simply that you looked at it together.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-I-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. How did you discuss teaching effectiveness and co-determine next steps?</h6><p>Analyze the joint discussion of successful practices and missed opportunities, then explain the next professional steps you co-determined. Include what the teacher will do, what you will do, the resources or support, and the evidence behind the plan.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-I-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. What does the teacher&#x27;s feedback reveal about your coaching?</h6><p>Report what the teacher found helpful and what was missing or unhelpful, then explain what that feedback reveals about your coaching strengths and growth. The current question explicitly asks for your interpretation, not just a transcript of the feedback exchange.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-3-3\"><h5>Rubric 3.3: Focused observation evidence</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.3 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Unrelated or vague notes, or absent analysis, do not support focused guidance.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>General impressions provide limited information for improvement.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Connect detailed, accurate notes and analysis to the selected elements and goal.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Document a missed opportunity or successful practice and its learning connection.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Examine whole-class, small-group, and individual needs and the teacher&#x27;s response or nonresponse.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 3.3</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate recognize, document, and analyze the volunteer teacher\u2019s teaching practice related to the selected CSTP element(s) and learning goals of the lesson?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.3 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate provides observational evidence that is unrelated to the CSTP element(s) and/or student learning goals of the observed lesson. OR Candidate\u2019s observation notes are too vague to provide CSTP- related guidance. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s qualities of teaching practice.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate provides evidence of the observed lesson that is mostly unrelated to the CSTP element(s) and/or student learning goals. Candidate documents evidence that may be CSTP related, but evidence is vague and/or too general and may not be useful to the volunteer teacher. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s qualities of teaching practice.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate accurately recognizes and documents observational evidence of essential teaching practices for the selected CSTP element(s) and content-specific student learning goals. Candidate\u2019s observation notes are detailed and clearly provide CSTP-specific guidance in a meaningful and useful way to the volunteer teacher. In their commentary, candidate clearly provides a clear analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s qualities of teaching practice.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate\u2019s observational evidence documented either a. missed teaching opportunities that would have supported students to meet the content-specific learning goals OR b. successful CSTP-related practices that the volunteer teacher did implement that positively impacted student learning during the lesson.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate documents how the volunteer teacher addresses whole-class, small-group, and individual student learning needs during the lesson and how they adapted or did not adapt their instruction to meet student needs as aligned to the learning goals of the lesson and the selected CSTP element(s).</p></section></div><p>Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) \u2022 Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting \u2022 Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) \u2022 Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 20</a>.</p></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-3-4\"><h5>Rubric 3.4: A learning conversation</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.4 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Irrelevant feedback, missing strengths or growth, or no analysis leaves the conversation unsupported.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>A vague evidence connection is not a rich learning conversation.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Demonstrate joint evidence-based interpretation and analyze your facilitation.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Connect the pre-meeting, observation, video, and student learning in depth.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Support the teacher&#x27;s capacity to evaluate instruction and assess learning.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 3.4</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to foster a learning conversation in a post- observation meeting using CSTP-focused observation evidence, lesson observation video, and student work with the volunteer teacher regarding strengths and area(s) for growth?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.4 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate provides inaccurate or irrelevant feedback to the volunteer teacher, making limited use of the observation and other collected evidence (e.g., lesson observation video, student work). OR No strengths or area(s) of growth for the volunteer teacher are discussed. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate provides the volunteer teacher with minimal CSTP-focused evidence or feedback, with limited discussion of findings. Candidate vaguely connects observation evidence, lesson observation video, or quality of student work to the volunteer teacher\u2019s strengths or area(s) for growth. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate fosters a thoughtful and productive collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher, citing the CSTP-specific observation evidence, the jointly viewed lesson observation video, and student work. Jointly with the volunteer teacher, candidate respectfully and supportively identifies teacher&#x27;s strengths and area(s) for growth based on gathered CSTP evidence and student work. In their commentary, candidate provides a clear analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate makes deep connections with the volunteer teacher among the pre- observation meeting considerations, the CSTP observation evidence, lesson observation video, and student learning as evidenced in the student work produced during the lesson.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate fosters a learning conversation that increases the volunteer teacher\u2019s capacity for self-evaluation of instruction and assessment of student learning.</p></section></div><p>Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) \u2022 Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting \u2022 Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) \u2022 Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 21</a>.</p></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-3-5\"><h5>Rubric 3.5: Shared growth steps</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.5 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>A candidate-assigned plan or missing support, evidence, or analysis is insufficient.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Occasional teacher input does not establish a developed joint plan.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Connect shared next steps and support to the CSTP evidence; analyze the process.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Attend specifically to individual students&#x27; learning needs.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Use specific evidence-based resources and detailed professional learning steps.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 3.5</summary><p>Essential Question: In partnership with the volunteer teacher, how does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to co- determine next steps for professional growth, including resources and additional coaching support based on the CSTP-related evidence during the post-observation meeting?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.5 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate solely determines the volunteer teacher\u2019s next steps for professional growth. OR There is little to no identification of resources and/or additional coaching support for the volunteer teacher. OR No relationship to CSTP-related evidence for next steps is discussed. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate occasionally allows the volunteer teacher to add their insights or perspective to the discussion on the teacher\u2019s next steps for professional growth. The conversation with the volunteer teacher lacks identification of resources and additional coaching support and/or the inclusion of CSTP- related evidence. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate participates in a collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher to co- determine next steps for professional growth, including resources and additional coaching support, that are clearly based on the CSTP- related evidence (observation feedback, lesson observation video, and/or student work). In their commentary, candidate provides a clear analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate and the volunteer teacher identify professional growth with specific attention to addressing individual students\u2019 learning needs for CSTP-related professional learning.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides specific evidenced-based resources to support the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional growth and engages the teacher in developing detailed next steps for professional learning and growth.</p></section></div><p>Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) \u2022 Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting \u2022 Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) \u2022 Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 22</a>.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-step\" id=\"ov-c3-step4\"><h3>Step 4: Reflect</h3><p>Use teacher feedback and cycle evidence to examine your coaching, the teacher&#x27;s ownership of the process, and equitable instructional leadership. Reflect on your own practice rather than evaluating only the teacher.</p><p class=\"ov-rubric-links\">Related rubrics: <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-3-6\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-3-6\">3.6</a>, <a href=\"#home/ov-rubric-3-7\" data-jump=\"ov-rubric-3-7\">3.7</a>. These apply across the related evidence, not as a separate score for each question.</p><section class=\"ov-part\" id=\"ov-c3-J\" data-part=\"J\"><h4>Part J: Reflection</h4><p>Reflect on your coaching partnership and equitable leadership. The teacher&#x27;s feedback is evidence to interpret and act on, not just a compliment to report.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Current Part J template</a></p><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-J-item-1\" data-question=\"1.\"><h6>1. What are your coaching strengths and growth needs, based on evidence and feedback?</h6><p>Evaluate your coaching using the teacher&#x27;s feedback and the cycle evidence. Explain why each strength or growth area matters for the agreed CSTP focus, and how your future approach will change. A hypothetical question is a proposed improvement, not something you actually asked.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-J-item-2\" data-question=\"2.\"><h6>2. How did two-way conversations support the teacher&#x27;s ownership of learning?</h6><p>Use evidence across the cycle to explain how the teacher retained voice and ownership in identifying strengths, needs, and next steps. Asking questions is not sufficient by itself: show how the teacher&#x27;s thinking influenced the work.</p></section><section class=\"ov-question\" id=\"ov-c3-J-item-3\" data-question=\"3.\"><h6>3. What did you learn about equitable leadership and coaching&#x27;s potential?</h6><p>Explain what this cycle taught you about equitable instructional leadership and the potential of coaching to support teacher growth. Connect your leadership stance to actual teacher feedback, classroom evidence, and student access. Potential impact should not be written as a guaranteed outcome.</p></section></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-3-6\"><h5>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on coaching</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.6 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Missing analysis, teacher feedback, or partnership evidence leaves reflection incomplete.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Cursory feedback use and vague evidence do not explain your capacity.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Connect specific coaching strengths and growth to feedback and cycle evidence.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Analyze the feedback extensively and explain a changed future approach.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Use research or evidence-based practices to explain support for adult learning and teacher development.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 3.6</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate analyze their capacity to conduct a CSTP-focused coaching and observation process, based on their experience in this coaching cycle and feedback from the volunteer teacher, and refer to evidence to demonstrate their ability to facilitate and maintain a coaching partnership?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.6 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not provide an analysis of their capacity to support teacher development through CSTP-focused coaching and/or observation processes. OR Candidate does not use the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback regarding their coaching skills. OR Candidate does not reflect on or refer to evidence of how they facilitated and maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate minimally describes strengths and/or areas for growth in CSTP-focused coaching and observation. Candidate\u2019s analysis provides cursory use of the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback. Candidate minimally reflects upon and/or vaguely refers to evidence of how they facilitated and/or maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate analyzes CSTP- focused coaching and observation strengths and clearly identifies areas for growth. Candidate\u2019s analysis clearly draws on the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback and other learning gained throughout the cycle. Candidate clearly reflects upon and refers to evidence of how they facilitated and maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides an extensive analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback to them regarding their coaching and observation abilities in planning and conducting the coaching cycle and discusses how they would change their future approach to coaching based on that feedback.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains how evidence-based practices or research related to coaching skills support teacher development and/or adult learning.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5C</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 24</a>.</p></section><section class=\"ov-rubric\" id=\"ov-rubric-3-7\"><h5>Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</h5><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.7 summary\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>No equity connection or no consideration of potential impact misses the purpose.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>A general statement of fairness is not a developed leadership reflection.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Explain your role, continuous improvement, and coaching&#x27;s potential.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Ground the reflection in specific cycle evidence or teacher feedback.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 and 4, plus: Use research to support coaching as an equitable approach to teacher and student learning.</p></section></div><details class=\"ov-official\"><summary>Complete official Rubric 3.7</summary><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate, informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focused on equitable leadership, reflect on their role as an instructional leader acting as an instructional coach and reflect on the potential impact of coaching?</p><div class=\"ov-levels\" role=\"list\" aria-label=\"3.7 official descriptors\"><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-1\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 1</h6><p>Candidate does not demonstrate an awareness of the connection between equitable leadership and instructional coaching. OR Candidate does not reflect upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-2\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 2</h6><p>Candidate vaguely reflects on and describes a minimal understanding of how instructional coaching and/or equitable leadership inform a continuous improvement mindset. Candidate minimally reflects upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-3\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 3</h6><p>Candidate, informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focused on equitable leadership, clearly reflects on their role as a school leader acting as an instructional coach. Candidate clearly reflects upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-4\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 4</h6><p>All of Level 3, plus: Candidate cites specific evidence from the cycle, drawing on the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback and/or other collected evidence, to support their reflection and analysis of their capacity to be an equitable leader and an instructional coach.</p></section><section class=\"ov-level ov-level-5\" role=\"listitem\"><h6>Level 5</h6><p>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites evidence-based practices or research that support instructional coaching and observation as a viable and equitable strategy to support teacher growth and benefit students.</p></section></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5C</p></details><p class=\"source-note\">Abbreviated teaching descriptions above; full descriptors govern. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=29\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current handbook, printed page 25</a>.</p></section></section><p><a href=\"#cycle/c3\">Continue to the detailed Cycle 3 guide and examples</a></p>"}};
